No, this isn't intended Inngest behavior. "Body exceeded 1mb limit" is not an Inngest message. It's the Next.js Pages Router API-route body parser error (default `sizeLimit` is 1mb). On each re-invocation, Inngest sends the function's memoized state, including earlier step outputs, in the request body to your app. Once a step returns more than 1MB, your framework rejects that request before the Inngest handler sees it. The function then fails instead of completing. PR #650 only changed how the dev UI displays large output, so it doesn't affect this path.

**Fix if you're on Next.js Pages Router:** raise the limit on the Inngest route:

```ts
// pages/api/inngest.ts
export const config = {
  api: { bodyParser: { sizeLimit: '4mb' } },
};
```

App Router doesn't have this limit. Other frameworks (Express's `express.json()` defaults to 100kb) have similar defaults.

The more robust approach is to avoid returning huge payloads from steps. Write the data to external storage (S3, a database) and return only a reference. The server also enforces its own state size limit [8], so very large outputs will eventually fail regardless of framework settings.

If you aren't using Next.js, tell us your framework and the full stack trace so we can find which layer is throwing.

