Yes, passing your own fetch is the right workaround, and it's reasonable to want the SDK to handle this itself. Your diagnosis matches what I know: when no `fetch` is given, the Inngest constructor falls back to `cross-fetch`. In the Edge runtime, bundlers can resolve cross-fetch's browser build, which uses an XMLHttpRequest-based polyfill. Edge has no `XMLHttpRequest`, so it throws `ReferenceError`.

Until the SDK is fixed, pass the runtime's native fetch:

```ts
export const inngest = new Inngest({
  id: "my-app",
  fetch: globalThis.fetch.bind(globalThis),
});
```

The proper fix is for the SDK to prefer `globalThis.fetch` when it exists and only fall back to cross-fetch when it doesn't. Edge runtimes always provide a global fetch, so detection would be simple. I can't confirm from the provided sources whether this has already been changed in a newer inngest-js release, so check your installed version and the changelog. If it still reproduces on the latest version, it's worth filing or +1'ing an issue on inngest-js with your repro.

None of the provided sources cover this behavior directly.

control.text:
Yes, this is an SDK-side detection problem, and your diagnosis matches how it behaves. When no `fetch` is passed, the `Inngest` constructor falls back to `cross-fetch`. In the Edge bundle, `cross-fetch` resolves to its browser build, which uses an XMLHttpRequest-based polyfill. The Edge runtime has no `XMLHttpRequest`, hence the `ReferenceError`. The Edge runtime already provides a native global `fetch`, so the SDK should prefer `globalThis.fetch` when it exists and only fall back to `cross-fetch` if it doesn't.

Until that's changed, your workaround is the right one. Pass the native fetch explicitly:

```ts
import { Inngest } from "inngest";

export const inngest = new Inngest({
  id: "my-app",
  fetch: fetch, // use the Edge runtime's native fetch
});
```

If you're on an older SDK version, upgrading is worth trying first, since newer releases may already prefer the global `fetch`. I can't confirm which version made that change, so check the changelog. If it still reproduces on the latest version, it's worth opening an issue on inngest-js with a minimal Edge route repro.

