control.text:
**Why it's on the client:** In v3, middleware isn't only for function runs. It also has hooks on the send path (e.g. `onSendEvent`, which can mutate or log events passed to `inngest.send`). Because of that, it's attached to the `Inngest` client, which is shared by both sending and function definitions. `serve()` only receives the functions, so it has no way to apply middleware to sends.

**Workaround:** use two clients with the same `id`:

```ts
// inngest/sendClient.ts (Edge-safe, no middleware)
export const inngestSend = new Inngest({ id: 'my-app' });

// inngest/client.ts (Node only, used by serve() and functions)
import { appsignalMiddleware } from './appsignal';
export const inngest = new Inngest({ id: 'my-app', middleware: [appsignalMiddleware] });
```

Import `inngestSend` in Next.js middleware and Next-Auth callbacks, and `inngest` in your `serve()` route. Events sent from the send-only client trigger functions on the full client as normal, because they share the app ID. You'll need to share event types or schemas between the two clients yourself.

Alternatively, keep one client and load `@appsignal/nodejs` lazily inside the middleware's `init`/hooks, guarded by `process.env.NEXT_RUNTIME === 'nodejs'`. Webpack may still try to resolve the import, so the two-client split is the more reliable option.

Per-`serve` or per-function middleware would be a reasonable feature request.
